{
 "lead": "Binary phase shift keying (BPSK) carries one bit in the phase of the carrier: cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t) for a 1 and its negative for a 0. The two signals are exact opposites (antipodal), which puts them as far apart as possible for a given energy, so BPSK has the lowest bit error probability of any binary scheme in white Gaussian noise. GPS, deep-space links and the control channels of mobile systems use it where power matters more than bandwidth.",
 "prereq": "You need: the correlator receiver and the Q-function (see baseband data transmission and detection), and <a href=\"#ask-and-fsk-generation-and-detection\">ASK and FSK</a> for comparison.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Waveform and generation",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>s(t) = ±√(2E<sub>b</sub>/T<sub>b</sub>) cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t),  0 ≤ t < T<sub>b</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "E<sub>b</sub> is the bit energy in joules (1 Ω reference), T<sub>b</sub> the bit time in s and f<sub>c</sub> the carrier in Hz; + sends bit 1 (phase 0) and − sends bit 0 (phase π). Squaring and integrating over a bit gives E<sub>b</sub>, which is why the amplitude is √(2E<sub>b</sub>/T<sub>b</sub>)."
    },
    {
     "type": "steps",
     "items": [
      "Convert the bits to bipolar NRZ: 1 → +1, 0 → −1.",
      "Multiply by the carrier √(2E<sub>b</sub>/T<sub>b</sub>)cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t) in a balanced (product) modulator.",
      "The output flips its phase by 180° at every change of data."
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The envelope is constant, so the power amplifier can run near saturation. Some syllabi call BPSK phase reversal keying (PRK)."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Coherent detection and the signal space",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The receiver multiplies r(t) = s(t) + w(t), where w(t) is white noise of two-sided density N<sub>0</sub>/2, by the unit-energy basis function φ(t) = √(2/T<sub>b</sub>)cos(2πf<sub>c</sub> t) and integrates over the bit:"
    },
    {
     "type": "formula",
     "text": "y = ∫ r(t)φ(t) dt = ±√E<sub>b</sub> + n",
     "where": "The integral runs over 0 to T<sub>b</sub>. y is in √J (volts × √seconds with a 1 Ω reference), not volts. n is Gaussian with mean 0 and variance N<sub>0</sub>/2."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The decision is the sign of y: y > 0 → 1, y < 0 → 0. On the signal-space line the points are +√E<sub>b</sub> and −√E<sub>b</sub>, a distance d = 2√E<sub>b</sub> apart, and the boundary is y = 0."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The local carrier must match the incoming phase. A squaring loop or a Costas loop recovers it from the received signal itself; both lock with a 180° ambiguity, which <a href=\"#non-coherent-detection-and-dpsk\">DPSK</a> removes. If the local phase is off by θ, the useful term becomes ±√E<sub>b</sub>·cos θ, so the effective energy falls to E<sub>b</sub> cos²θ."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Error probability and bandwidth",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "A 1 is misread when the noise pulls y below zero, which needs n < −√E<sub>b</sub>. With σ = √(N<sub>0</sub>/2),"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>P<sub>b</sub> = Q(√E<sub>b</sub>/σ) = Q(√(2E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>))</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "N<sub>0</sub> is the one-sided noise density in W/Hz and Q(x) = (1/√(2π))∫ e<sup>−u²/2</sup> du from x to ∞. In erfc form P<sub>b</sub> = ½erfc(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)), since Q(x) = ½erfc(x/√2)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Coherent BFSK and OOK have Q(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)) (see <a href=\"#ask-and-fsk-generation-and-detection\">ASK and FSK</a>), so for the same P<sub>b</sub> they need twice the E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>: BPSK is 3 dB better. The cost is carrier recovery."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "With rectangular bits the power spectral density is sinc² shaped around f<sub>c</sub>, S(f) ∝ sinc²((f − f<sub>c</sub>)T<sub>b</sub>). Its first nulls are at f<sub>c</sub> ± R<sub>b</sub>, so the null-to-null bandwidth is 2R<sub>b</sub> and the bandwidth efficiency 0.5 b/s/Hz. There is no carrier line, because the bipolar data has zero mean."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A BPSK link runs at R<sub>b</sub> = 1 Mb/s with received E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 dB. (a) Find P<sub>b</sub>. (b) How many errors are expected in 10<sup>8</sup> bits, and per second? (c) The recovered carrier is 25° off. Find the new P<sub>b</sub>.",
   "steps": [
    "E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10<sup>10/10</sup> = 10.",
    "Argument √(2 × 10) = √20 = 4.472; P<sub>b</sub> = Q(4.472) = 3.87 × 10<sup>−6.</sup>",
    "Errors in 10<sup>8</sup> bits = 10<sup>8</sup> × 3.87 × 10<sup>−6</sup> = 387. At 10<sup>6</sup> bit/s that is 3.87 errors per second on average.",
    "With θ = 25°: cos²25° = 0.8214, so the effective E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10 × 0.8214 = 8.214 (9.15 dB).",
    "New argument √(2 × 8.214) = 4.053; P<sub>b</sub> = Q(4.053) = 2.53 × 10<sup>−5</sup>, about 6.5 times worse."
   ],
   "answer": "(a) 3.87 × 10<sup>−6</sup>; (b) about 387 errors in 10<sup>8</sup> bits, 3.87 per second; (c) 2.53 × 10<sup>−5</sup> with a 25° phase error."
  },
  {
   "question": "A BPSK receiver needs P<sub>b</sub> = 10<sup>−6.</sup> The noise density is N<sub>0</sub> = 4.0 × 10<sup>−21</sup> W/Hz (thermal noise at 290 K) and R<sub>b</sub> = 1 Mb/s. Find the required E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> and the minimum received power in dBm. How much more would coherent BFSK need?",
   "steps": [
    "Q(x) = 10<sup>−6</sup> gives x = 4.753 (from a Q table).",
    "2E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = x², so E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 4.753²/2 = 11.30, which is 10.53 dB.",
    "E<sub>b</sub> = 11.30 × 4.0 × 10<sup>−21</sup> = 4.52 × 10<sup>−20</sup> J.",
    "Received power P = E<sub>b</sub>·R<sub>b</sub> = 4.52 × 10<sup>−20</sup> × 10<sup>6</sup> = 4.52 × 10<sup>−14</sup> W.",
    "In dBm: 10 log<sub>10</sub>(4.52 × 10<sup>−14</sup>/10<sup>−3</sup>) = −103.4 dBm.",
    "Coherent BFSK needs E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = x² = 22.59 (13.54 dB): 3.01 dB more, so −100.4 dBm."
   ],
   "answer": "E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 10.53 dB; minimum received power −103.4 dBm (45.2 fW). Coherent BFSK needs 3 dB more, −100.4 dBm."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "BPSK is Q(√(2E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)); Q(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)) is coherent BFSK or OOK. Dropping the 2 costs 3 dB.",
  "Q(x) and erfc differ by a factor and a √2: Q(x) = ½erfc(x/√2), so P<sub>b</sub> = ½erfc(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>)). Mixing the two forms is the commonest numeric slip.",
  "The null-to-null bandwidth of rectangular BPSK is 2R<sub>b</sub>, not R<sub>b</sub>.",
  "A carrier phase error θ scales the amplitude by cos θ and the energy by cos²θ; at 90° BPSK fails completely.",
  "The correlator output is in √J, not volts; the noise on it has variance N<sub>0</sub>/2, not N<sub>0</sub>."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "State the two BPSK signal points and the decision boundary.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "+√E<sub>b</sub> and −√E<sub>b</sub> on one axis; the boundary is y = 0."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why is BPSK called antipodal, and why does that make it the best binary scheme?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "The two signals are negatives of each other, so for energy E<sub>b</sub> each their distance 2√E<sub>b</sub> is the largest possible; P<sub>b</sub> depends only on that distance."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Find P<sub>b</sub> for BPSK at E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = 7 dB.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "7 dB = 5.012; √(2 × 5.012) = 3.166; P<sub>b</sub> = Q(3.166) = 7.73 × 10<sup>−4.</sup>"
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A BPSK link sends 2 Mb/s with rectangular pulses. Find the null-to-null bandwidth and efficiency.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "B = 2R<sub>b</sub> = 4 MHz; efficiency 2/4 = 0.5 b/s/Hz."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "Write P<sub>b</sub> of BPSK with erfc.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "P<sub>b</sub> = ½erfc(√(E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>))."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "BPSK and coherent BFSK must both reach P<sub>b</sub> = 10<sup>−5.</sup> Which needs less E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub>, and by how much?",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "Q(x) = 10<sup>−5</sup> gives x = 4.265. BPSK: E<sub>b</sub>/N<sub>0</sub> = x²/2 = 9.09 (9.59 dB). BFSK: x² = 18.19 (12.60 dB). BPSK saves 3.01 dB."
  },
  {
   "level": "analyze",
   "q": "A 30° carrier phase error appears. Find the energy loss in dB.",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "cos²30° = 0.75; loss = −10 log 0.75 = 1.25 dB."
  }
 ],
 "beyond": "Not for the exam. BPSK remains the reference for power efficiency: GPS civil signals, deep-space telemetry and the most robust LTE and 5G control channels use it or its two-carrier form. Practical receivers track phase with Costas loops or digital decision-directed loops. Search next: Costas loop, squaring loop, phase ambiguity.",
